A||||8||SibERThuG||dimmer switch transistor??||||5||
Z||000000||SibERThuG||10-21-2000||01:39 AM||||My transitor that runs between my radio and my headlight switch seems to be no good. My wiring is good, my fuses are good, headlight switch is good, but gauge lights don't. So, the only thing I can think of is this transistor thingy. Anyways, my question is will a transistor from any year, any model fit and work in my Formula?? Are these transistors universal??? If not and I can't get one from a junkyard, how much are these new?? Sorry this is so long... Please help!! Also, my car is for sale... check out the mall... <P>------------------<BR>Robbie<BR>88' Formula||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-21-2000||12:40 PM||||I do not know, but I strongly suspect that any dash light dimmer transistor from any GM car (if it has one) will work.  There's nothing special about Fiero's dash lights, so I bet GM would use the same design for multiple car lines. <P>Before you go and purchase any replacment parts, though, it's quite possible the thing just needs to be cleaned.  Try:  <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/techtips.htm#Dashlights" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/techtips.htm#Dashlights</A><P>Click on "Dashlights don't work?"<P>Good luck     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||10-21-2000||05:28 PM||||Actually the white stuff vargus is cleaning off the heatsink is the heat sink compound. That should be replaced. you can get it at radio shack. The plastic shim he's talking about is an insulator to prevent a short. I really wish he'd redo that article or remove it. It's fairly screwed up.<P>Could also be the dimmer wheel on the dash. the contact in there may be dirty broken or worn out.<P>If the transtisor itself is bad you can get suitable replacements from most electronics suppliers. I's have to check it and the schmatics but I think it's just a high amp NPN transistor in a TO3 case. The exact type isn't critical as long as it has the right terminal placement and high enough current rating.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-21-2000||09:37 PM||||Before you replace it, check the connections where the pins on the back of the transistor plug into the socket. They're spring contacts, and will gradually fatigue and not grip the pins. There is a possibility that you can remove the two screws, unplug the transistor, bend the pins just a hair, and reinstall it. I had this same problem with a Firebird. Twice. The second time, I just put a dab of solder on the pins. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-22-2000||12:37 AM||||just taking the socket off and putting it back on can solve problems too. the transistor pins are solder tinned if they are plated at all. working the contats will usually scrub off any crud that builds up. (Which was probably what really solved Paul Vargus' problem.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||SibERThuG||10-22-2000||12:44 AM||||I tried to clean the transistor. It didn't work. Could it just be no good? I used alcohol to clean it, but I also cracked the plastic shim. I'll get a new one, see if that works. If not, I'll buy an Autozone license plate light and rig it. Oh well! <P>------------------<BR>Robbie<BR>88' Formula||||reg||4
Z||000006||theogre||10-22-2000||01:17 PM||||Yes, like a said above the transistor could be bad but you need to check the dimmer wheel on the dash too.<P>A cracked insulator is a non issue as long as the transisor can't short to the heatsink. If it's broken off a chunk,,, You should be able to get new insulators along with heat sink compound at radio shack. (The white stuff between the transistor and heat sink belongs there. Heatsink compound makes the heat flow out of the transistor better.)<P>I'll have to dig one of these out next time I go to the scrap yard and see how it's wired and what transistors can replace it. I expect there's a bunch that will work as good or better. Radio Shack used to carry a "darlington" transistor that would handle 20 amps. (The more capacity the transistor has above the actual load the cooler it will run.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||10-22-2000||01:44 PM||||Ogre, thanks for the tip on the Paul Vargyas article.  Never had troubles with the dimmer myself (yet); if/when I do, I hope I remember what you told us here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>SibERThuG, if you're shopping for a replacement transistor, the local Radio Scrap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> here, happens to have a 15A NPN power transistor in a TO-3 case for about $2.  Just for comparison purposes - don't know if it would work for this application.  Hopefully, you can find one in salvage yard for the same or less.<P>||||reg||10
Z||000008||theogre||10-22-2000||02:41 PM||||It will work electrically. just an issue of if the terminals pin out the same. Most of them are the same but every so often you run into one type with the pins reversed. 15A is definatly heavy enough. I think the circuit only has a 7 amp fuse.||||reg||1
